# 10. THREE DEVELOPMENT PHASES

Determine the next three phases from the actual repository state.

## PHASE 1 — AUDIT, REALITY CHECK & FOUNDATION

- Audit all existing features.
- Find boilerplate, mock, and demo functionality.
- Inventory real and fake data sources.
- Identify incomplete business logic.
- Audit the database and persistence.
- Map all frontend routes and backend APIs.
- Identify missing integrations.
- Correct broken contracts.
- Fix important inconsistencies.
- Simplify the architecture.
- Establish integration and E2E test infrastructure.
- Create a prioritized real-implementation backlog.
- Start fixing critical foundation issues immediately.

**Deliverable:** An accurate, improved foundation with a concrete map of all real implementation gaps.

## PHASE 2 — REAL PRODUCT IMPLEMENTATION

This is the highest-priority implementation phase.

- Replace fake production data.
- Complete actual business logic.
- Complete database persistence.
- Implement missing backend operations.
- Wire frontend to real APIs.
- Complete important user workflows.
- Fix CRUD.
- Implement actual search and filtering.
- Persist settings.
- Complete authentication and authorization.
- Replace simulated status and metrics.
- Complete integrations where credentials exist.
- Improve UI/UX.
- Consolidate reusable functionality.
- Implement selected high-value innovations.

**Deliverable:** A genuinely working, data-driven application rather than a visual prototype.

## PHASE 3 — INTEGRATION, E2E & PRODUCTION HARDENING

- Complete integration.
- Resolve remaining contract drift.
- Remove remaining fake production functionality.
- Verify data integrity.
- Complete critical E2E journeys.
- Validate real database persistence.
- Test permissions.
- Test failure and recovery behavior.
- Improve reliability.
- Harden security.
- Optimize performance.
- Complete documentation.
- Prepare and execute production release.

**Deliverable:** A fully integrated, tested, data-driven, production-ready product.

Do not consider a phase finished simply because code was written.

