# 16. MANDATORY VERSION BUMP & PRODUCTION RELEASE

**PRODUCTION RELEASE IS A REQUIRED DELIVERABLE.**

Development without a verified production release is incomplete.

This prompt authorizes routine repository-approved commits, version bumps, integrations, Git tags, stable GitHub Releases, and production deployments using established infrastructure.

## 16.1 Version Audit

Inspect:

- Authoritative version.
- Package manifests.
- Lockfiles.
- Monorepo policy.
- Existing tags.
- Previous releases.
- Application metadata.
- Changelog.

Follow existing Semantic Versioning conventions when adopted.

## 16.2 Version Bump

1. Determine previous version.
2. Determine correct next version.
3. Document rationale.
4. Update authoritative version.
5. Synchronize relevant package versions.
6. Update lockfiles.
7. Update metadata.
8. Update CHANGELOG.md.
9. Verify consistency.
10. Commit release changes.

Never reuse an existing version or create duplicate tags.

## 16.3 Production Readiness

Before publishing:

- All three phases completed.
- Critical features genuinely implemented.
- No unresolved critical fake production functionality.
- Real data sources verified.
- Important database persistence verified.
- Frontend/backend wiring completed.
- Critical E2E workflows pass.
- Quality gates pass.
- Security risks reviewed.
- Production configuration validated.
- Migrations and rollback procedures prepared.

A build alone is insufficient.

## 16.4 Release Sequence

**IMPLEMENT → INTEGRATE → TEST → E2E VERIFY → HARDEN → VERSION BUMP → CHANGELOG → COMMIT → TAG → GITHUB RELEASE → PRODUCTION DEPLOYMENT → VERIFY**

Execute:

1. Final repository audit.
2. Complete critical defects.
3. Verify real data and production behavior.
4. Run unit, integration, and E2E tests.
5. Verify production build.
6. Validate production configuration.
7. Verify migration compatibility.
8. Bump version.
9. Update changelog.
10. Commit changes.
11. Integrate under repository governance.
12. Create Git tag.
13. Publish stable GitHub Release.
14. Trigger production deployment.
15. Verify actual deployment.
16. Run production smoke tests.
17. Verify health and critical workflows.
18. Confirm deployed version.

## 16.5 Failure Recovery

When an operation fails:

- Inspect logs.
- Find root cause.
- Fix it.
- Re-run checks.
- Rebuild.
- Retry.
- Verify.

Prioritize production recovery if the application becomes unhealthy.

Never fake deployment success, bypass essential security, expose secrets, or destroy production data.

Do not fabricate external credentials or permissions.

## 16.6 Release Acceptance Checklist

- [ ] Three phases complete.
- [ ] Critical boilerplate replaced.
- [ ] Production demo data eliminated or properly isolated.
- [ ] Real business logic implemented.
- [ ] Real persistence verified.
- [ ] Critical frontend/backend wiring complete.
- [ ] Important contracts validated.
- [ ] E2E workflows pass.
- [ ] Tests and quality gates pass.
- [ ] Version bumped.
- [ ] Packages synchronized.
- [ ] Changelog updated.
- [ ] Changes committed.
- [ ] Git tag created.
- [ ] Stable GitHub Release published.
- [ ] Production deployment succeeds.
- [ ] Production health verified.
- [ ] Critical smoke tests pass.
- [ ] Production version confirmed.

Any unmet mandatory criterion means the release is incomplete.

